opening='The head-of-hall reverend is the monastery’s presiding abbot or teacher as named from the community’s institutional side.'
body='Pure rules put the title into invitations, announcements, and appointments: a formal notice invites a head-of-hall reverend, a hall attendant announces that he has cancelled evening interview, and a rector cites his instruction when appointing an officer. Reception language asks the head-of-hall reverend at Wuzu to expound before the assembly. Tianyi Yihuai credits such a teacher with close formation and encouragement, while Hongjue Min uses the title when announcing the death of the present monastery’s presiding teacher. The Chan bend is institutional rather than symbolic: the teacher of public encounter is named here as the person who presides over the hall and whose word organizes its communal offices and events.'
